Keep raw own-slot lookup available to resolving definitions.

The earlier native-interceptor guard must reject pure public/optimizing lookups
that cannot call a handler, but NativeLookupOwnPropertyNoResolve is the engine's
explicit raw-slot operation. It is used by NativeDefineProperty when a builtin
constructor is installed with JSPROP_RESOLVING. Rejecting that internal lookup
silently made intercepted global allocation return null without an exception.
Public pure lookups retain CheckResolve/CheckMayResolve and still decline.
This corrects our extension; it is not an upstream SpiderMonkey defect.

--- a/js/src/vm/NativeObject-inl.h
+++ b/js/src/vm/NativeObject-inl.h
@@ -735,7 +735,8 @@
 static MOZ_ALWAYS_INLINE bool NativeLookupOwnPropertyInline(
     JSContext* cx, typename MaybeRooted<NativeObject*, allowGC>::HandleType obj,
     typename MaybeRooted<jsid, allowGC>::HandleType id, PropertyResult* propp) {
-  if constexpr (allowGC == NoGC) {
+  if constexpr (allowGC == NoGC &&
+                resolveMode != LookupResolveMode::IgnoreResolve) {
     if (!id.isEmbeddingPrivateName() &&
         obj->getClass()->getNativePropertyInterceptors()) {
       return false;
